Q: Pygame event issues A: <p>You try to run the same function with different objects - <code>event</code> and <code>list</code> - but your code was created only for single <code>event</code>.</p>
<p>Frankly, I don't know why you need to run it with <code>move_log</code> but ...</p>
<p>If you need to run for list then you may create separate function</p>
<pre><code>    def process_list(self, events_list):
        for event in events_list:
            self.process_events(event)
</code></pre>
<pre><code>#Bruce.process_events(move_log)
Bruce.process_list(move_log)
</code></pre>
<p>If you don't want another function then you can use <code>isinstance()</code> to check if you run function with single <code>event</code> or <code>list</code> and execute different code.</p>
<p>(by the way: you have to compare <code>event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN</code> and <code>event.key == pygame.K_w</code>)</p>
<pre><code>    def process_events(self, event):
        if isinstance(event, list):
           for item in event:
              # ... code ...
        else:
           if event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN:
               if event.key == pygame.K_w:
                   print(&quot;YAY&quot;)

</code></pre>
<p>If <code>move_log</code> is list of events then you can convert single <code>event</code> to list - to recude code</p>
<pre><code>    def process_events(self, event):
        if not isinstance(event, list):
           event = [event]

        for item in event:
           if event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN:
               if event.key == pygame.K_w:
                   print(&quot;YAY&quot;)
</code></pre>

Q: Crossfade of two images via CSS, but all I get is a blank page A: <p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/cross-fade" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/cross-fade</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>The cross-fade() CSS function can be used to blend two or more images at a defined transparency. It can be used for many basic image manipulations, such as tinting an image with a solid color or highlighting a particular area of the page by combining an image with a radial gradient.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/cross-fade#browser_compatibility" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/cross-fade#browser_compatibility</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Requires a vendor prefix or different name for use.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/2234" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/2234</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>[css-images] cross-fade() syntax doesn't match implemented webkit-cross-fade() #2234</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a demo showing how you might use <code>cross-fade</code> consistently despite its very limited support:</p>
<p><sub>*I used different pictures because yours weren't CORS free and frankly speaking they were riding the trolling side of the coast</sub></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.cfade {
  /* fallback for non-supporting browsers */
  background-image: url(https://picsum.photos/id/10/200/200);

  /* Chrome, Safari, Opera (prefixed legacy syntax) */
  background-image: -webkit-cross-fade(
    url(https://picsum.photos/id/10/200/200),
    url(https://picsum.photos/id/20/200/200),
    50%
  );

  /* Future spec syntax (currently limited support) */
  background-image: cross-fade(
    url(https://picsum.photos/id/10/200/200) 50%,
    url(https://picsum.photos/id/20/200/200) 50%
  );

  width: 200px;
  height: 200px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;div class="cfade"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;hr&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;For reference:&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;img src="https://picsum.photos/id/10/200/200"&gt;
  &lt;img src="https://picsum.photos/id/20/200/200"&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<hr />
<p>This is another strategy that will be more safely supported and it will still behave like you would expect with <code>crossfade</code> blending 2 pictures 50%-50%:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.cfade {
  position: relative;
  width: 200px;
  height: 200px;
}

.cfade .img1,
.cfade .img2 {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-size: cover;
}

.cfade .img1 {
  background-image: url(https://picsum.photos/id/10/200/200);
}

.cfade .img2 {
  background-image: url(https://picsum.photos/id/20/200/200);
  opacity: 0.5;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;div class="cfade"&gt;
    &lt;div class="img1"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="img2"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How do I solve `malloc(): corrupted top size`? A: <p>For starters there are numerous memory leaks at least due to the function <strong>cleanLine</strong></p>
<pre><code>char* cleanLine(char* d, FILE* fp) {
    char* c = strdup(d);
    //....
    return c;
}
</code></pre>
<p>where there is used function <strong>strdup</strong> that dynamically allocates memory that is not freed in the program. Moreover the address of the allocated memory can be lost.</p>
<p>And the address of the initial value of the pointer <strong>instruction</strong> that also points to dynamically allocated memory also is lost due to this statement and moreover can be equal to <strong>NULL.</strong></p>
<pre><code>instruction = cleanLine(instruction, fp);
</code></pre>
<p>As a result such calls as that</p>
<pre><code>fgets(instruction, 100, fp) 
</code></pre>
<p>or using the pointer further in the program invoke undefined behavior.</p>
<p>Looking at the code I do not see a reason why you are allocating memory dynamically for a character array where you are reading records from the file. It would be much simpler and safer to declare a character array</p>
<pre><code>char instruction[100];
</code></pre>
<p>Also separating calls of <strong>fgets</strong> between <strong>main</strong> and  the function <strong>cleanLine</strong> is a bad design. It would be much better to write a function that returns to <strong>main</strong> a next command or <strong>NULL</strong> from the file. At present within the while loop</p>
<pre><code> while (fgets(instruction, 100, fp) != NULL) {
     instruction = cleanLine(instruction, fp);
</code></pre>
<p>the pointer <strong>instruction</strong> can be a <strong>NULL</strong> pointer returned by the function <strong>cleanLine</strong>.</p>
<p>And the function <strong>instructionType</strong></p>
<pre><code>int instructionType(char* c) {
    if (*c == '(') return TYPE_L;
    else if (*c == '@') return TYPE_A;
    else return TYPE_C;
}
</code></pre>
<p>can also invoke undefined behavior if the value of the first character of the passed string is not one of the listed values in the if-else statements. Instead of these macros</p>
<pre><code>#define TYPE_A 0
#define TYPE_C 1
#define TYPE_L 2
</code></pre>
<p>It is better to declare an enumeration adding one more element that signals a bad command.</p>
<p>And also you need to check whether the file was opened successfully.</p>
<p>So my advice is redesign your program entirely.</p>
<p>For example you could define a function named like <strong>getNextInstruction</strong> the following way</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;
#incluse &lt;string.h&gt;

const size_t N = 100;

char * getNextInstruction( char *buffer, FILE *fp )
{
    char comment = '/';
    bool found = false;
    char *first = NULL;

    while ( !found &amp;&amp; fgets( buffer, N, fp ) != NULL )
    {
        size_t n = strcspn( buffer, &quot;\n&quot; );
        buffer[ n ] = '\0'; 

        first = buffer;

        while ( isblank( ( unsigned char )*first ) ) ++first;

        if ( *first != '\0' &amp;&amp; *first != comment )
        {
            char *last = buffer + n;

            while ( isblank( ( unsigned char )last[-1] ) ) --last;

            *last = '\0';

            found = true;
        }
    }

    return found ? first : NULL;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And in <strong>main</strong> you need to declare a pointer that will accept a returned value from the function.</p>
<p>For example</p>
<pre><code>char buffer[N];
char *instruction;

while ( ( instruction = getNextInstruction( buffer, fp ) ) != NULL )
{
    // check the instruction and do something else if required
    // as for example to call strdup for the string 
    // pointed to by instruction 
}
</code></pre>

Q: Trying to get explosion to disappear after laser intersects with rectangle object A: <p>The Timer continues to fire even after you remove the rectangle from the Array.</p>
<p>You need to stop the Timer:</p>
<pre><code>                if (rects.get(1).intersects(rects.get(3))) {
                    ((Timer)e.getSource()).stop();
                    visible = !visible;
                    rects.remove(3);
                    repaint();
                }
</code></pre>
